[REDACTED_LOCAL_PATH]"schema_version": "1.0", "session_id": "9b817448-eaef-47c3-a08b-397b71600687", "team_id": "contest2026_329_dela", "github_login": "pjjuihj", "tool": "claude-code", "seq": 1512, "ts": "2026-08-15T02:47:09.772Z", "role": "tool", "tool_name": "<result>", "tool_call_id": "call_30a7d223cd034081afdc2696", "input": null, "output": "1\t# SF32LB52-LCHSPI-ULP Board Support for openvela\n2\t\n3\t[ English | [简体中文](README_zh-cn.md) ]\n4\t\n5\t## Introduction\n6\t\n7\tThis directory provides openvela support for the SiFli **SF32LB52-LCHSPI-ULP**\n8\treference development board on the `dev-ai-contest-2026` branch.\n9\t\n10\tThe board pairs the SF32LB52 wearable / IoT MCU with a 1.85\" 390×450\n11\tAMOLED panel (CO5300 + BV6802W) over QSPI, an FT6146 capacitive touch\n12\tcontroller, and an AW32001 charger. The same PCB is also re-spun by\n13\t立创开发板 (LCKFB) as the *Huangshan Pi*; see\n14\t[`../lckfb_huangshan_pi`](../lckfb_huangshan_pi) for the LCKFB variant.\n15\t\n16\tFor board hardware details and the official getting-started guide, see\n17\tthe upstream SiFli documentation:\n18\t\n19\t- [SF32LB52 product page](https://www.sifli.com/productinfo/1093720.html)\n20\t- [SF32LB52-LCHSPI-ULP Wiki](https://wiki.sifli.com/board/sf32lb52x/SF32LB52-Lchspi-ULP.html)\n21\t\n22\t> ⚠️ **Branch dependency**\n23\t>\n24\t> This board overlay only builds on the `dev-ai-contest-2026` branch of\n25\t> `open-vela/nuttx` and `open-vela/vendor_sifli`. Building it from\n26\t> `trunk` or `dev` will fail because the chip-side dependencies are not\n27\t> yet upstream.\n28\t\n29\t## Directory Structure\n30\t\n31\t```\n32\tvendor/sifli/boards/sf32lb52/sf32lb52_lchspi_ulp/\n33\t├── Kconfig                   # board-level Kconfig options\n34\t├── include/board.h           # clock / GPIO / MTD board definitions\n35\t├── include/drv_io.h          # SDK-style IO abstraction headers\n36\t├── src/                      # board bring-up sources\n37\t│   ├── sifli_ap.c            # board_late_initialize → bringup\n38\t│   ├── bsp_pinmux.c          # pinmux for UART / I2C / QSPI / LCD\n39\t│   ├── bsp_lcd_tp.c          # LCD power-up + touch reset glue\n40\t│   ├── bsp_power.c           # AW32001 charger I2C2 + NOR DPD\n41\t│   ├── bsp_init.c            # MPU + early board hooks\n42\t│   ├── sf32lb52_buttons.c    # KEY1 / KEY2 button driver\n43\t│   └── ...\n44\t├── configs/nsh/              # NSH defconfig (LVGL + LCD + touch)\n45\t└── scripts/                  # link script (XIP @ 0x12010000)\n46\t```\n47\t\n48\t## Supported Peripherals\n49\t\n50\t| Peripheral | Driver | Device node |\n51\t|-----------|--------|-------------|\n52\t| 1.85\" 390×450 AMOLED (CO5300, QSPI) | `co5300` + `sf32lb_lcd` | `/dev/lcd0`, `/dev/fb0` |\n53\t| FT6146 capacitive touch (I2C1)      | `ft6146`                | `/dev/input0` |\n54\t| AW32001 charger (I2C2 @0x49)        | `bsp_power.c` (kernel)  | `/dev/i2c1` (raw) |\n55\t| KEY1 / KEY2 push buttons            | `sf32lb52_buttons`      | `/dev/buttons` |\n56\t| ADC0 (8 channels, 12-bit)           | `sf32lb_adc`            | `/dev/adc0` |\n57\t| RTC                                 | `sf32lb_rtc`            | `/dev/rtc0` |\n58\t| Watchdog                            | `sf32lb_iwdg`           | `/dev/watchdog0` |\n59\t| Hardware timer                      | `sf32lb_tim`            | `/dev/timer0` |\n60\t| Internal NOR (16 MB, MPI2 bus)      | `sf32lb_flash` MTD      | `/dev/config0` |\n61\t| USB CDC ACM device                  | `cdcacm`                | `/dev/ttyACM0` |\n62\t| UART1 console (CH340N, 1 Mbps)      | `sf32lb_uart`           | `/dev/console`, `/dev/ttyS0` |\n63\t\n64\t## GPIO Pin Map (nsh defconfig)\n65\t\n66\t| Function | GPIO |\n67\t|----------|------|\n68\t| UART1 RX / TX (console)         | PA18 / PA19 |\n69\t| Touch I2C1 SDA / SCL            | PA33 / PA37 |\n70\t| Touch INT / RST                 | PA41 / PA09 |\n71\t| Charger I2C2 SDA / SCL          | PA11 / PA10 |\n72\t| QSPI LCD CS / CLK / TE          | PA03 / PA04 / PA02 |\n73\t| QSPI LCD D0 / D1 / D2 / D3      | PA05 / PA06 / PA07 / PA08 |\n74\t| LCD reset / VADD_EN             | PA00 / PA01 |\n75\t| VSYS / VCC_3V3 power gates      | PA38 / PA26 |\n76\t| KEY1 / KEY2                     | PA34 / PA43 |\n77\t| RGB LED (PWM)                   | PA32 |\n78\t\n79\tThe 22-pin LCD FPC pinout follows the SiFli SF32LB52 reference design.\n80\tLCKFB-branded modules use the same wiring (LCKFB silkscreen labels\n81\tQSPI D0 as `PB05`, but it is the same physical net as PA05).\n82\t\n83\t## Build\n84\t\n85\t```bash\n86\tcmake -B cmake_out/sf32lb52_lchspi_ulp -S \"$PWD/nuttx\" -GNinja \\\n87\t  -DBOARD_CONFIG=../vendor/sifli/boards/sf32lb52/sf32lb52_lchspi_ulp/configs/nsh \\\n88\t  -DEXTRA_FLAGS=\"-Wno-cpp -Wno-deprecated-declarations\"\n89\tcmake --build cmake_out/sf32lb52_lchspi_ulp\n90\t```\n91\t\n92\tThe build produces `cmake_out/sf32lb52_lchspi_ulp/nuttx.bin` (~1.5 MB), to\n93\tbe flashed to the on-package NOR at offset `0x12010000`.\n94\t\n95\t## Flash\n96\t\n97\tThe board uses the SiFli ROM bootloader (SFBL) followed by a flat XIP\n98\timage at `0x12010000`. **No separate bootloader or partition table.**\n99\t\n100\tThe CH340N USB-UART bridge has its **RTS line wired to the SF32LB52\n101\treset signal** (active low). This is deliberate: it lets PC-side tools\n102\ttoggle hardware reset without a physical button press, mirroring the\n103\tauto-reset pattern that ESP-IDF / `esptool.py` use on ESP32 boards.\n104\t**Automated flashing and self-testing rely on this wiring** — do not\n105\tcut or pull-up the trace if you intend to use `sftool` from CI.\n106\t\n107\t```bash\n108\t# Erase + write nuttx.bin to NOR @ 0x12010000, then soft-reset into NSH\n109\tsftool -c SF32LB52 -p /dev/ttyUSB0 -b 1000000 \\\n110\t       --before default_reset --after soft_reset \\\n111\t       write_flash cmake_out/sf32lb52_lchspi_ulp/nuttx.bin@0x12010000\n112\t```\n113\t\n114\tIf `sftool` reports `Failed to connect to the chip`, the SoC's ROM\n115\tbootloader missed the ~2 s `ATSF32` listen window after RTS reset —\n116\tre-plug the USB cable and retry. If the board boot-loops printing only\n117\t`SFBL`, the AMOLED panel is browning out the USB rail; use a 5 V/2 A\n118\twall charger or a battery, not a high-impedance laptop USB-A port.\n119\t\n120\t## First Boot & Quick Tests\n121\t\n122\tOpen the UART1 console at 1 000 000 8N1 (no flow control). Because of\n123\tthe RTS-to-RST wiring, terminal choice matters:\n124\t\n125\t```bash\n126\t# Recommended for interactive use — keeps RTS deasserted at open():\n127\tpicocom -b 1000000 --noreset --lower-rts --lower-dtr /dev/ttyUSB0\n128\t```\n129\t\n130\t`minicom` will reset the SoC once at connect (RTS asserted on `open()`)\n131\tbut stays running afterwards. `screen` and `cu` cannot deassert RTS and\n132\tkeep the chip held in reset — avoid them.\n133\t\n134\tAfter RTS reset you should see SFBL then NuttX boot:\n135\t\n136\t```\n137\tSFBL\n138\tABCD\n139\tADC calibration data missing, use defaults\n140\tINFO: NOR MTD registered at /dev/config0 (offset=2464 blocks=1024)\n141\t\n142\tNuttShell (NSH)\n143\tnsh> ls /dev\n144\t/dev:\n145\t adc0      buttons   config0   console   fb0       gpio0     gpio1\n146\t gpio2     i2c0      i2c1      input0    lcd0      pwm0      ram0\n147\t rtc0      spi1      timer0    ttyACM0   ttyS0     urandom   watchdog0\n148\tnsh> uname -a\n149\tNuttX 0.0.0 ... arm sf32lb52_lchspi_ulp\n150\t```\n151\t\n152\tPer-peripheral hand-test (all from `nsh>`):\n153\t\n154\t```\n155\tfree                    # confirm 8 MB OPI-PSRAM in user heap\n156\tps                      # task list\n157\tadc -n 1                # one-shot ADC sample\n158\tdate                    # RTC read\n159\ti2c dev 0x10 0x77       # FT6146 ACKs at 0x38\n160\ti2c dev 0x40 0x4f       # AW32001 ACKs at 0x49 on /dev/i2c1\n161\tbuttons 5               # press KEY1 / KEY2 within 5 events\n162\tfb                      # 6 nested rectangles to AMOLED\n163\tlvgldemo widgets        # full LVGL widgets demo + FT6146 touch\n164\tostest                  # NuttX kernel regression suite\n165\t```\n166\t\n167\t## Customising the Configuration\n168\t\n169\t```bash\n170\tcd cmake_out/sf32lb52_lchspi_ulp\n171\tninja menuconfig                 # tweak interactively\n172\tninja savedefconfig\n173\tcp defconfig \\\n174\t   ../../vendor/sifli/boards/sf32lb52/sf32lb52_lchspi_ulp/configs/nsh/defconfig\n175\t```\n176\t\n177\tTo create an additional config variant, copy `configs/nsh/` to\n178\t`configs/<your-name>/` and pass the new path to `-DBOARD_CONFIG=`.\n179\t\n180\t## Automated Test Mode\n181\t\n182\tThe RTS-to-RST wiring is what enables fully automated CI / pytest /\n183\texpect harnesses. The test driver behaves as a remote reset button —\n184\tno physical interaction required:\n185\t\n186\t```python\n187\timport serial, time\n188\tser = serial.Serial('/dev/ttyUSB0', 1_000_000, timeout=0.5)\n189\tser.rts = True;  time.sleep(0.05)        # assert hardware reset\n190\tser.rts = False; time.sleep(0.5)         # release, board boots\n191\tser.read_until(b\"nsh> \")                 # wait for NSH prompt\n192\tser.write(b\"ostest\\r\\n\")\n193\tout = ser.read_until(b\"PASSED\\n\", )\n194\tassert b\"PASSED\" in out\n195\t```\n196\t\n197\t## Known Limitations\n198\t\n199\t1. The CO5300 panel does not always reply to QSPI ID query reliably on\n200\t   USB-only power; the LCD driver logs `[co5300] ReadID=0x0 expected\n201\t   0x331100, init anyway` and proceeds. This is benign — the panel\n202\t   accepts the SLPOUT / DISPON sequence and renders correctly.\n203\t2. `/dev/uorb` is empty — sensor uORB integration is queued for a future\n204\t   PR. The charger is exposed as raw `/dev/i2c1` only.\n205\t3. SD card is not wired on this board.\n206\t4. RTS-to-RST means a Linux serial open with default `termios` keeps\n207\t   the SoC in reset. Use `picocom --lower-rts --lower-dtr` (or the\n208\t   pyserial recipe above) for interactive console access.\n209\t\n210\t## License\n211\t\n212\tAll files in this directory are licensed under Apache-2.0 (SPDX\n213\tidentifier `Apache-2.0`); see individual files for full headers.\n214\t", "is_error": false}

